<!--
    kuna re-bytecode-reader-return-discarded: a call whose cspec output rule
    asked for a register PAIR got no output at all, and both halves rendered
    as locals the function never assigns.

    FuncCallSpecs::buildOutputFromTrials (fspec.cc:5777) moves the single used
    output trial to the CALL; upstream also handles two, and kuna shipped that
    arm as a stub.  The x86-64 System V cspec returns any 16-byte aggregate in
    RAX:RDX and says so with its join_dual_class output rule, so a caller that
    reads both registers puts two active trials on the call.  With the arm
    stubbed the INDIRECT creations standing for "the callee wrote something
    here" survived and the returned value had no definition anywhere.

    The vector is sub_9d9c0 of a stripped gcc 9.4 QuickJS interpreter
    (challenge 673da52e9b533b4c22bd2eeb), whose JSValue-returning call to
    sub_875e0 at 0x9d9d8 rendered as a bare statement followed by a cast of an
    unassigned rdx local.  Reduced to one call and assembled from
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/callretpair_x86_64.s:

        401020  push %rbx
        40102b  call read_value            ; returns { void *u; long tag; }
        401030  mov  %rax,%rbx             ; the payload half
        401033  cmp  $0x6,%edx             ; the tag half, JS_TAG_EXCEPTION

    read_tag_clobber is the control: no_payload provably never writes rdx, so
    the bounded callee decode refuses the pair and the call keeps no output in
    BOTH passes.

    Phase: P4 output prototype, option callretpair off|on.  Pass 1 (off) pins
    the bug: a bare read_value() call and an unassigned rdx local.  Pass 2 (on,
    the shipped default) pins the join output and both halves.  The control
    does not move.
-->
