<script>
  <com>option callretpair off</com>
  <com>lo fu read_tag</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu read_tag_clobber</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>option callretpair on</com>
  <com>lo fu read_tag</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>lo fu read_tag_clobber</com>
  <com>decompile</com>
  <com>print C</com>
  <com>quit</com>
</script>
<stringmatch name="CALLRETPAIR #1: off, the call is a bare statement and its JSValue is discarded" min="1" max="1">^  read_value\(a0,a1,0x10,1\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLRETPAIR #2: on, the call gains the 16-byte join output the cspec output rule asked for" min="1" max="1">v1 = read_value\(a0,a1,0x10,1\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLRETPAIR #3: on, both halves are SUBPIECEs of the call output instead of locals with no definition" min="1" max="1">return \*SUB168\(v1,0\) \+ SUB168\(v1,8\);</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLRETPAIR #4: the unassigned rdx local survives in three of the four renderings, and is gone only where the pair formed" min="3" max="3">int8 v2; // rdx</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLRETPAIR #5: the control keeps its bare call in BOTH passes, because the bounded callee decode proves no_payload never writes rdx" min="2" max="2">^  no_payload\(a0,a1,7\);$</stringmatch>
<stringmatch name="CALLRETPAIR #6: on, the recovered return stops being the garbage pointer the discarded half left behind" min="1" max="1">int8 read_tag\(unsigned long a0,unsigned long a1\)</stringmatch>
